The directory is empty, so I'll show you the implementation pattern directly. Here's how to set up global error handling with `IExceptionHandler`:

**1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourApp.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // Let the default handler produce a safe 500

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title,  // Don't use exception.Message — may leak internals
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

**2. Register in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
// Services
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

// Middleware (before endpoint routing)
app.UseExceptionHandler();   // Catches exceptions, invokes IExceptionHandler
app.UseStatusCodePages();    // Ensures non-exception error codes also get ProblemDetails
```

**How it works:**

- `AddProblemDetails()` configures the default handler to return RFC 7807 JSON for unhandled exceptions (500) with **no** sensitive details exposed.
- Your custom `ApiExceptionHandler` runs first — if it recognizes the exception type, it writes the mapped status code + ProblemDetails and returns `true` (handled).
- If it returns `false`, the built-in handler produces a generic 500 ProblemDetails response.

No try-catch needed in endpoints — just throw normally:

```csharp
app.MapGet("/api/items/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    // These exceptions are automatically mapped by the middleware
    throw new KeyNotFoundException();       // → 404
    throw new ArgumentException("...");     // → 400
    throw new InvalidOperationException(); // → 409
    // Any other exception                  // → 500 (safe)
});
```
